tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:2:5: note: cost of `gram`: M²·N + M·N flops, 1 allocation of M² floats
  |
2 | def gram(A: Matrix[N, M]) -> Matrix[M, M]:
  |     ^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 16·M·N bytes read, 8·M² bytes written

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:3:5: note: `gram` costs M²·N + M·N flops, within `2 * N * M^2`
  |
3 |     cost flops <= 2 * N * M^2
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = note: proven from the static cost model, for every size from 1 up
  = note: the cost is that of the optimized program (spec "Performance contracts")

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:4:12: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta](A, A)`
  |
4 |     return Aᵀ @ A
  |            ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
  = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[ta]` reads it transposed, so the N·M element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
  = note: because exact: `matmul[ta]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
  = note: because the flops are unchanged: 2·N·M²

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:4:12: note: [LA-GRAM-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta,alg=syrk](A, A)`
  |
4 |     return Aᵀ @ A
  |            ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `structure` pass
  = note: because A is multiplied by its own transpose: entries (i, j) and (j, i) sum the same products (`aₚᵢ·aₚⱼ` and `aₚⱼ·aₚᵢ`, and multiplication commutes) in the same order
  = note: because one triangle is computed and mirrored: N·M² + N·M flops instead of 2·N·M²
  = note: because exact: the result is bit for bit the general product's, and bitwise symmetric (its type gains the Symmetric tag)

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:6:5: note: cost of `step`: 2·N flops, 1 allocation of N floats
  |
6 | def step(v: Vector[N], w: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
  |     ^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 16·N bytes read, 8·N bytes written

