  |
9 |     cost bytes_written == 8 * N
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = note: proven from the static cost model, for every size from 1 up
  = note: the cost is that of the optimized program (spec "Performance contracts")

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:10:12: note: [FUSE-001] `v + 2 * w` is rewritten to `fused(2, w, v)`
   |
10 |     return v + 2 * w
   |            ^^^^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `fuse` pass
   = note: because fused 2 elementwise operations into one kernel; saves 1 temporary array of shape Vector[N]
   = note: because every intermediate result is used exactly once, by the next operation, and every array operand is a Vector[N]
   = note: because 2 of the operations can fail (`mul`, `add`); the kernel reports the failure of the first of them in program order that fails at any element, which is the error the separate operations gave

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:10:12: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise kernel runs on 8 threads once it has at least 2048 elements
   |
10 |     return v + 2 * w
   |            ^^^^^^^^^
   = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
   = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
   = note: because 2 of its operations can fail (`mul`, `add`): if chunks fail, the failing operation that comes first in program order is reported, at its lowest failing element, which is the error a sequential run gives
   = note: because native code runs it on 8 threads too from 262144 elements (a compiled kernel computes an element so fast that splitting pays only from there), in the same chunks, and resolves a failure by the same rule, so its result and error are the same

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:10:12: note: [MEM-001] `v + 2 * w` reuses `w`'s buffer: no allocation for this result
   |
10 |     return v + 2 * w
   |            ^^^^^^^^^
   = note: `w` is not used again after this on any path, and has the shape of the result
   = note: applied by the `lifetimes` analysis of the SIR interpreter

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_proven.sky:12:5: note: cost of `solve_spd`: N³/3 + 2·N² flops, 2 allocations (N² + N floats in all)
   |
12 | def solve_spd(A: PositiveDefinite Matrix[N, N], b: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
   |     ^^^^^^^^^
   = note: memory traffic: 8·N² + 8·N bytes read, 8·N² + 8·N bytes written

